{:edb/db-before-t 1143 :edb/db-after-t 1144 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534456 50 #inst "2026-10-11T21:07:27.695Z" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059969 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+z=9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059969 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+z=9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186059969 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+0y+z=9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059969 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+y+z=4 \\\\ x+0y+z=9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063384 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186069272 1074 "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186069272 1074 "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186069312 1071 "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186069312 1071 "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\nI. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n\nII. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186069334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067174 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186069334 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186069334 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186088430 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186088430 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186092709 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092709 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3+z=0 \\\\ 5y-1+3x=2z+1 \\\\ 3z+2x-1=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind  $x+y+z.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186092709 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+z=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-0+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092709 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+z=3 \\\\ 3x+5y-2z=2 \\\\ 2x-2y+3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-0+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(15-4)+(-6-10) \\\\  & =22-16 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & -16 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -13 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=4-4-5=-5.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186097349 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+2z=8 \\\\  & -2x+2y-3z=-11 \\\\  & x+z=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186097349 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+2z=8 \\\\  & -2x+2y-3z=-11 \\\\  & x+z=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186097349 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z=8 \\\\ y-z=1 \\\\ z=5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=5$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5)=1\\,⟹\\,y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=6$ and $z=5$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(6)+2(5)=8\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=4$, $y=6$, and $z=5.$" 1319413953