) & =P((X,Y)=(1,4)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,6)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(1,4)+f(1,6)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186162304 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,1).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186162304 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |  |" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186167510 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186167510 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186176496 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186176496 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\nI. $(0,5]$\n\nII. $[1,5]$\n\nIII. $[0,5]$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186198717 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186198717 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186198717 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186198717 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,1]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,i]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186198778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195379 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186198778 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7a/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c