186045417 false] [17592186247387 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5}).$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186247387 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\frac{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\frac{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{5}) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!}{2!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186247387 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5}) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!}{2!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186247389 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186247389 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186250247 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186250247 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x