.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186256650 1071 "Evaluate $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6}).$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186256650 1074 "First, note that $(\\frac{7}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{1!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186256650 1074 "First, note that $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{1!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186259601 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186259601 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186259753 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $162+108+72+48+⋯$\n- $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$\n- $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186259753 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $162+108+72+48+⋯$\n\nII. $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$\n\nIII. $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186262644 1074 "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186262644 1074 "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186262652 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the outcome of the spinner. Then the support is $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{6}:$\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=f(4)=f(5)=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(5)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+5(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+1+2+3+4+5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186262652 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the outcome of the spinner. Then the support is $S={0,1,2,3,4,5},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{6}:$\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=f(4)=f(5)=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(5)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+5(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+1+2+3+4+5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186262685 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x