d}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186265976 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards picked until a red one is chosen. Since there are $26$ red cards in a $52$ cards deck, the chance of choosing a red card is $\\frac{26}{52}.$ Thus, the number of chosen cards before getting a red card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{26}{52}=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{1-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{2-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{5-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)+(0.5)(0.5)+(0.5)^{2}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & =[1+0.5+(0.5)^{2}+(0.5)^{3}+(0.5)^{4}](0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186265976 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards picked until a red one is chosen. Since there are $26$ red cards in a $52$ cards deck, the chance of choosing a red card is $\\frac{26}{52}.$ Thus, the number of chosen cards before getting a red card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{26}{52}=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{1-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{2-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{5-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)+(0.5)(0.5)+(0.5)^{2}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & =[1+0.5+(0.5)^{2}+(0.5)^{3}+(0.5)^{4}](0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186266004 1074 "Since $\\overset{―}{kv}=\\overset{―}{v},$ we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{kv} & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{v} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{4} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-5-\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{k} \\\\ (-5+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{k}=-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ (-5+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k}=18-40\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{k}=-5+7\\text{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain the true statement\n\n$$\n(-5+\\text{i})(-5+7\\text{i})=18-40\\text{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\overset{―}{-5+7\\text{i}}=-5-7\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, $\\text{Re}(k)=-5.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186266004 1074 "Since $\\overset{―}{kv}=\\overset{―}{v},$ we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{a