{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186329821 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186329825 1071 "Given that that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, has a jump disc