2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.23),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.77)}^{x-1}(0.23), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(0.77)}^{3}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{4}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{5}(0.23) \\\\  & ≈0.2481.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186360760 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.23),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.77)}^{x-1}(0.23), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & =P(X\\in {4,5,6}) \\\\  & =P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(0.77)}^{3}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{4}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{5}(0.23) \\\\  & ≈0.2481.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362209 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{7-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{7-6} \\\\  & =21(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{2}+7(0.9)^{6}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.4960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362209 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{7-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{7-6} \\\\  & =21(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{2}+7(0.9)^{6}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.4960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362210 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in \\{7\\}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362210 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in {7}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362313 1071 "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a node" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362313 1071 "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=-2$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=-2$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=2$ is a node" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362319 1071 "![](images/4a/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362319 1071 "![](images/4a/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=4$ is a source.\n\nII. $y=2$ is a node.\n\nIII. $y=-1$ is a sink." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362322 1071 "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186362322 1071 "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=3$ is a node.\n\nII. $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nIII. $y=-1$ is a source." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362399 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186362399 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186386048 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3y=-x \\\\ 2y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186386048 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3y=-x \\\\ 2y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186386048 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186386048 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186386209 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y-z=11 \\\\ 