s every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/4d/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186389916 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times N\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e6816767cd2010fca1b8991ed9c5e0c42d3a6d6466d78025534bc8362a174b9.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any natural number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/4d/4d0fe261e790520e6a8786e6f024d5b9cb7cd54c18242103a1f4b77f09653abc.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times N$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7e54dd973126e3179e79b1ba1a26f4a7c1c541ca764b14c4ccef73b4452509e.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390326 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-1,2\\}\\times \\{-3,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390326 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390326 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-1,2\\}\\times \\{-3,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-1,-3),(-1,1),(2,-3),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390326 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-1,-3),(-1,1),(2,-3),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390333 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390333 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390333 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=\\{0.5\\}\\times [0,0.125]=\\{(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](images/47/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390333 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}={0.5}\\times [0,0.125]={(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](images/47/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390381 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=0 \\\\  & y+2z=0 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390381 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=0 \\\\  & y+2z=0 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390388 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390388 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390694 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=1 \\\\ 8x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390694 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=1 \\\\ 8x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390711 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390711 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186390718 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=46 \\\\ 8x+y=-23\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186390718 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=46 \\\\ 8x+y=-23\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186391617 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ 2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186391617 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ 2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186392101 1071 "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186392101 1071 "Find the left-sided limit at $x=0$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x\\le 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186392128 1071 "Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186392128 1071 "Consider the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186392481 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 3x+9y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186392481 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 3x+9y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186392481 1074 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186392481 1074 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393215 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x=-2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186393215 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x=-2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393215 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186393215 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393299 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=4 \\\\  & 2x-y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186393299 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=4 \\\\  & 2x-y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393306 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-4y=6 \\\\  & 4x-7y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186393306 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-4y=6 \\\\  & 4x-7y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393306 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=3 \\\\  & y=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-2$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-2)=3\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-2.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 true] [17592186393306 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=3 \\\\  & y=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-2$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-2)=3\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-2.$" 13194139534456 17592186045417 false] [17592186393355 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=4 \\\\ -3x+6y=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination